## Positioning (what the study claims over this work)
1. PPI++ is optimal only inside the class "g = a + b f(X)". With labels, unlabeled data and f fixed,
   the remaining freedom is *how the labels are used to build the control variate g* and *how the
   interval is formed from it*. The classical semi-supervised literature shows the optimal g is
   E[Y | Z]; PPI papers never measure how far PPI++ is from that floor on the standard datasets,
   and do not study when learning g from n labels pays for itself and when it hurts coverage.
2. The study gives (a) a computable per-dataset headroom (floor vs PPI++ vs classical), (b) a
   cross-fitted calibrated interval that attains the floor asymptotically with valid coverage,
   (c) a map of regimes: gain / no gain / invalid for all methods.
3. It does not claim a new asymptotic efficiency principle; it claims an honest, reproducible
   answer to "how much shorter can it be", including where the answer is "not at all".

## Notes from review (idea-review #3)
- Floor derivation (pooled Z over all n+N records; V* = sigma_res^2/n + V_g/(n+N)) is derived in the Brief; verify the bound statement
  against a primary source before the paper cites it as "the" semiparametric bound, and do not use "first"/"new" for the principle or the question.
- Stratified PPI is run post hoc (labels fixed), unlike its design; Cross-PPI and active inference are excluded for the reasons above.
- Validity of the instrument needs only an L2-stable limit of the calibrator because the propensity is known (MCAR); consistency is needed only for the width.

- Efficiency-bound source (idea-review #3): Robins, Rotnitzky & Zhao, JASA 89:846-866 (1994), and Tsiatis, *Semiparametric Theory and Missing Data*, Springer 2006 (existence confirmed through a citing abstract; not read in full).

